1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:03,580
Every second, the Sun turns
about six hundred million

2
00:00:03,590 --> 00:00:05,410
tonnes of hydrogen into helium.

3
00:00:06,250 --> 00:00:09,740
In the process, around four
million tonnes of matter

4
00:00:09,750 --> 00:00:12,770
simply disappears, turned into
pure energy.

5
00:00:13,450 --> 00:00:16,930
That energy is sunlight, and it
powers almost all life on Earth.

6
00:00:17,583 --> 00:00:21,013
For seventy years, scientists
have been trying to build a
small

7
00:00:21,023 --> 00:00:22,263
star here on Earth.

8
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:26,230
If they succeed, it could give
us almost limitless clean

9
00:00:26,240 --> 00:00:26,770
energy,

10
00:00:26,780 --> 00:00:29,060
from fuel found in seawater.

11
00:00:29,540 --> 00:00:31,730
This is the secret behind
nuclear fusion,

12
00:00:31,740 --> 00:00:33,360
and why it's so hard.

13
00:00:34,983 --> 00:00:38,303
For most of history, nobody knew
what made the Sun shine.

14
00:00:38,983 --> 00:00:40,903
If it were a giant lump of
burning coal,

15
00:00:41,463 --> 00:00:43,563
it would have burned out in a
few thousand years.

16
00:00:44,417 --> 00:00:47,677
The first clue came from
Einstein's famous equation:

17
00:00:48,117 --> 00:00:49,697
E equals m c squared.

18
00:00:50,317 --> 00:00:53,727
A tiny amount of mass can be
turned into an

19
00:00:53,737 --> 00:00:55,297
enormous amount of energy.

20
00:00:56,083 --> 00:00:59,013
In nineteen twenty, the British
astronomer Arthur

21
00:00:59,023 --> 00:01:02,153
Eddington noticed that a helium
atom weighs

22
00:01:02,163 --> 00:01:05,453
slightly less than the four
hydrogen atoms it could be

23
00:01:05,463 --> 00:01:06,263
built from.

24
00:01:06,750 --> 00:01:09,920
He suggested that stars shine by
fusing hydrogen into

25
00:01:09,930 --> 00:01:10,360
helium,

26
00:01:10,370 --> 00:01:13,050
and turning that missing mass
into energy.

27
00:01:13,833 --> 00:01:17,283
Many physicists argued that the
Sun simply wasn't hot

28
00:01:17,293 --> 00:01:18,093
enough for that.

29
00:01:18,533 --> 00:01:21,943
Eddington's reply was famous: he
told his critics to go

30
00:01:21,953 --> 00:01:23,353
and find a hotter place.

31
00:01:24,250 --> 00:01:27,400
In nineteen thirty nine, Hans
Bethe worked out the exact

32
00:01:27,410 --> 00:01:30,110
nuclear reactions that power the
stars.

33
00:01:30,790 --> 00:01:32,730
He later won the Nobel Prize for
it.

34
00:01:34,233 --> 00:01:36,133
So why is fusion so hard?

35
00:01:36,553 --> 00:01:38,913
Because atomic nuclei hate each
other.

36
00:01:39,583 --> 00:01:42,763
Every nucleus carries a positive
electric charge.

37
00:01:43,403 --> 00:01:46,363
As two nuclei get close, they
push each other away,

38
00:01:46,863 --> 00:01:47,763
harder and harder.

39
00:01:48,500 --> 00:01:51,830
Only if they collide fast
enough, can they get close
enough

40
00:01:51,840 --> 00:01:55,140
for the strong nuclear force to
grab hold and fuse them.

41
00:01:55,833 --> 00:01:57,293
Speed means heat.

42
00:01:57,873 --> 00:02:01,283
In the Sun's core, the
temperature is about fifteen
million

43
00:02:01,293 --> 00:02:01,843
degrees,

44
00:02:01,853 --> 00:02:04,763
and the crushing weight of the
Sun squeezes

45
00:02:04,773 --> 00:02:05,753
nuclei together.

46
00:02:06,500 --> 00:02:09,950
Even at fifteen million degrees,
almost no nuclei are fast

47
00:02:09,960 --> 00:02:10,760
enough.

48
00:02:10,940 --> 00:02:14,040
They fuse thanks to a quantum
trick called tunnelling,

49
00:02:14,200 --> 00:02:17,480
which lets a lucky few slip
through the barrier between
them.

50
00:02:18,083 --> 00:02:20,383
On Earth, we can't recreate that
pressure.

51
00:02:21,043 --> 00:02:24,513
So we need to go much hotter:
about a hundred and fifty
million

52
00:02:24,523 --> 00:02:25,323
degrees,

53
00:02:25,423 --> 00:02:27,963
ten times hotter than the centre
of the Sun.

54
00:02:28,750 --> 00:02:31,130
At that temperature, matter
becomes plasma:

55
00:02:31,710 --> 00:02:34,430
a glowing soup of bare nuclei
and electrons.

56
00:02:35,110 --> 00:02:37,550
And no material container could
hold it.

57
00:02:37,950 --> 00:02:39,330
It would vaporise the walls.

58
00:02:40,250 --> 00:02:43,130
The best fuel is two heavy forms
of hydrogen:

59
00:02:43,670 --> 00:02:46,640
deuterium, which can be
extracted from ordinary

60
00:02:46,650 --> 00:02:47,600
seawater,

61
00:02:47,610 --> 00:02:49,970
and tritium, which can be made
from lithium.

62
00:02:50,750 --> 00:02:54,200
When they fuse, they make
helium, and a fast neutron
carrying

63
00:02:54,210 --> 00:02:55,250
most of the energy.

64
00:02:56,000 --> 00:02:59,217
Humans first released fusion
energy in nineteen fifty

65
00:02:59,227 --> 00:02:59,510
two,

66
00:02:59,520 --> 00:03:01,240
in the hydrogen bomb.

67
00:03:01,780 --> 00:03:03,160
That was fusion out of control.

68
00:03:03,700 --> 00:03:05,940
The challenge since then has
been to tame it.

69
00:03:07,483 --> 00:03:10,623
The answer to holding plasma
came from the Soviet Union.

70
00:03:11,223 --> 00:03:14,573
In the early nineteen fifties,
physicists Igor Tamm

71
00:03:14,583 --> 00:03:17,753
and Andrei Sakharov proposed
trapping it with

72
00:03:17,763 --> 00:03:18,623
magnetic fields.

73
00:03:19,583 --> 00:03:22,933
Charged particles spiral around
magnetic field

74
00:03:22,943 --> 00:03:23,743
lines.

75
00:03:24,103 --> 00:03:27,593
Bend those lines into a ring,
and the plasma can

76
00:03:27,603 --> 00:03:29,863
circle forever without touching
the walls.

77
00:03:30,750 --> 00:03:34,180
In America, the physicist Lyman
Spitzer designed a

78
00:03:34,190 --> 00:03:37,220
different magnetic bottle, a
twisted ring called the

79
00:03:37,230 --> 00:03:38,030
stellarator.

80
00:03:38,310 --> 00:03:41,700
Germany's Wendelstein seven X,
switched on

81
00:03:41,710 --> 00:03:44,430
in twenty fifteen, is a modern
version.

82
00:03:45,250 --> 00:03:48,560
Holding plasma steady has been
compared to trying to squeeze

83
00:03:48,570 --> 00:03:51,470
a balloon, or hold jelly with
rubber bands.

84
00:03:52,190 --> 00:03:55,220
Squeeze one place, and it bulges
out somewhere

85
00:03:55,230 --> 00:03:56,030
else.

86
00:03:56,583 --> 00:03:59,853
The design was called a tokamak,
a Russian

87
00:03:59,863 --> 00:04:02,913
acronym for a toroidal chamber
with magnetic

88
00:04:02,923 --> 00:04:03,723
coils:

89
00:04:04,123 --> 00:04:05,923
a doughnut-shaped magnetic
bottle.

90
00:04:06,667 --> 00:04:09,817
In nineteen sixty eight, a
Soviet tokamak

91
00:04:09,827 --> 00:04:13,087
called T-three reached around
ten million degrees.

92
00:04:14,067 --> 00:04:15,447
Western scientists were
sceptical,

93
00:04:16,187 --> 00:04:19,517
so a British team flew to Moscow
with lasers to

94
00:04:19,527 --> 00:04:20,327
measure it.

95
00:04:20,487 --> 00:04:21,527
The Soviets were right.

96
00:04:22,333 --> 00:04:25,683
Soon, tokamaks were being built
all over

97
00:04:25,693 --> 00:04:26,493
the world.

98
00:04:26,917 --> 00:04:30,247
In nineteen fifty eight,
countries declassified their

99
00:04:30,257 --> 00:04:32,637
fusion research at a conference
in Geneva.

100
00:04:33,317 --> 00:04:36,347
That same year, Britain's ZETA
machine was briefly

101
00:04:36,357 --> 00:04:39,827
celebrated for achieving fusion,
before scientists

102
00:04:39,837 --> 00:04:42,137
realised the measurements had
been misread.

103
00:04:43,817 --> 00:04:44,677
The biggest was JET,

104
00:04:45,397 --> 00:04:47,677
the Joint European Torus, in
England.

105
00:04:48,197 --> 00:04:51,227
In nineteen ninety seven, it
produced sixteen

106
00:04:51,237 --> 00:04:53,217
megawatts of fusion power.

107
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:57,030
JET ran for forty years at
Culham, near

108
00:04:57,040 --> 00:04:57,510
Oxford,

109
00:04:57,520 --> 00:05:00,770
from nineteen eighty three until
twenty twenty

110
00:05:00,780 --> 00:05:01,110
three,

111
00:05:01,120 --> 00:05:04,060
and trained a whole generation
of fusion scientists.

112
00:05:05,333 --> 00:05:08,053
But that took more power to run
than it produced.

113
00:05:08,793 --> 00:05:11,943
The holy grail is to get more
energy out of the fuel than you

114
00:05:11,953 --> 00:05:12,853
put in to heat it.

115
00:05:13,500 --> 00:05:16,950
In twenty twenty three, in its
final experiments before

116
00:05:16,960 --> 00:05:17,860
closing,

117
00:05:18,220 --> 00:05:20,630
JET set a world record: sixty
nine

118
00:05:20,640 --> 00:05:23,750
megajoules of energy in five
seconds,

119
00:05:23,760 --> 00:05:26,480
from a fraction of a milligramme
of fuel.

120
00:05:27,167 --> 00:05:28,727
There's another way to do
fusion.

121
00:05:29,287 --> 00:05:32,737
Instead of holding plasma
steady, you can crush a tiny

122
00:05:32,747 --> 00:05:36,117
pellet of fuel so fast that it
fuses before it can

123
00:05:36,127 --> 00:05:36,927
fly apart.

124
00:05:37,583 --> 00:05:40,693
At the National Ignition
Facility in California,

125
00:05:40,703 --> 00:05:43,953
a hundred and ninety two giant
lasers fire

126
00:05:43,963 --> 00:05:46,603
at a capsule the size of a
peppercorn.

127
00:05:47,250 --> 00:05:50,160
On the fifth of December, twenty
twenty two,

128
00:05:50,170 --> 00:05:53,560
for the first time, the fusion
reactions released more

129
00:05:53,570 --> 00:05:56,480
energy than the laser light
delivered to the target:

130
00:05:56,490 --> 00:05:59,920
three point one five megajoules
out, from

131
00:05:59,930 --> 00:06:01,510
two point oh five in.

132
00:06:02,167 --> 00:06:04,687
It was a historic milestone
called ignition.

133
00:06:05,367 --> 00:06:08,397
The lasers themselves still used
far more electricity

134
00:06:08,407 --> 00:06:09,907
than the fusion produced.

135
00:06:10,687 --> 00:06:14,057
But it proved the physics works,
and the team has since beaten

136
00:06:14,067 --> 00:06:14,867
that result.

137
00:06:16,567 --> 00:06:19,777
Now, thirty five countries are
building the biggest

138
00:06:19,787 --> 00:06:22,977
tokamak ever, ITER, in the south
of

139
00:06:22,987 --> 00:06:23,787
France.

140
00:06:24,250 --> 00:06:27,560
Its magnets will be cooled to
minus two hundred and sixty nine

141
00:06:27,570 --> 00:06:28,370
degrees,

142
00:06:28,910 --> 00:06:31,510
just a few degrees above
absolute zero,

143
00:06:32,170 --> 00:06:34,940
while the plasma inside reaches
a hundred and fifty

144
00:06:34,950 --> 00:06:35,890
million.

145
00:06:36,330 --> 00:06:39,570
Some of the coldest and hottest
places in the solar system,

146
00:06:39,990 --> 00:06:40,870
a few metres apart.

147
00:06:41,667 --> 00:06:45,107
When it's finished, the machine
will weigh about twenty three

148
00:06:45,117 --> 00:06:48,567
thousand tonnes, around three
times as much as the Eiffel
Tower.

149
00:06:49,333 --> 00:06:52,483
ITER is designed to produce ten
times more

150
00:06:52,493 --> 00:06:55,433
fusion power than it uses to
heat its plasma.

151
00:06:56,173 --> 00:06:59,583
It has faced long delays, and
full operations are

152
00:06:59,593 --> 00:07:01,804
now planned for the late twenty
thirties.

153
00:07:02,333 --> 00:07:03,133
Meanwhile,

154
00:07:03,293 --> 00:07:06,593
dozens of private companies are
racing to build smaller,

155
00:07:06,933 --> 00:07:09,583
cheaper reactors, using powerful
new

156
00:07:09,593 --> 00:07:12,193
superconducting magnets and
clever designs.

157
00:07:13,167 --> 00:07:16,617
In twenty twenty one, a team
from MIT and the

158
00:07:16,627 --> 00:07:19,877
company Commonwealth Fusion
Systems tested a new

159
00:07:19,887 --> 00:07:23,107
superconducting magnet that
reached a field of twenty tesla,

160
00:07:23,587 --> 00:07:24,827
a record for its kind.

161
00:07:25,667 --> 00:07:29,137
And in early twenty twenty five,
tokamaks held hot plasma

162
00:07:29,147 --> 00:07:31,957
steady for almost eighteen
minutes in China,

163
00:07:31,967 --> 00:07:35,097
and twenty two minutes in
France, new records

164
00:07:35,107 --> 00:07:36,187
for staying power.

165
00:07:37,900 --> 00:07:39,280
Why does it matter so much?

166
00:07:39,840 --> 00:07:42,360
Because fusion fuel is
incredibly concentrated.

167
00:07:43,333 --> 00:07:46,503
Fusion reactions release
millions of times more

168
00:07:46,513 --> 00:07:49,153
energy per kilogramme than
burning coal,

169
00:07:49,553 --> 00:07:50,633
oil or gas.

170
00:07:51,313 --> 00:07:54,243
A pineapple-sized amount of fuel
could match

171
00:07:54,253 --> 00:07:56,233
thousands of tonnes of coal.

172
00:07:57,000 --> 00:07:58,660
It produces no carbon dioxide.

173
00:07:59,480 --> 00:08:02,980
And unlike today's nuclear
power, it can't melt down.

174
00:08:03,500 --> 00:08:06,810
If anything goes wrong, the
plasma simply cools

175
00:08:06,820 --> 00:08:08,160
and the reaction stops.

176
00:08:09,083 --> 00:08:12,373
It does create some
radioactivity, as neutrons hit
the

177
00:08:12,383 --> 00:08:15,593
reactor walls, but far less
long-lived waste

178
00:08:15,603 --> 00:08:16,403
than fission.

179
00:08:17,083 --> 00:08:19,183
The engineering problems are
still huge.

180
00:08:19,863 --> 00:08:23,093
The fast neutrons slowly damage
the walls of the machine,

181
00:08:23,103 --> 00:08:26,413
and the tritium fuel must be
made inside the reactor

182
00:08:26,423 --> 00:08:26,973
itself,

183
00:08:26,983 --> 00:08:27,783
from lithium.

184
00:08:28,583 --> 00:08:31,713
Scientists like to joke that
fusion power is always

185
00:08:31,723 --> 00:08:32,943
thirty years away.

186
00:08:33,283 --> 00:08:36,633
But the experiments of the last
few years have brought it closer

187
00:08:36,643 --> 00:08:37,843
than ever before.

188
00:08:39,483 --> 00:08:42,703
For billions of years, fusion
has lit up the universe.

189
00:08:43,523 --> 00:08:46,953
Every atom of carbon in your
body, and the oxygen you

190
00:08:46,963 --> 00:08:47,293
breathe,

191
00:08:47,303 --> 00:08:50,463
was forged by fusion inside
ancient stars.

192
00:08:51,333 --> 00:08:54,673
Now, we're trying to light that
same fire here on Earth.
